"Selecta Mathematica (2022) 28:10 (https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10353965)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Keywords Kreweras words/walks · Promotion · Evacuation · Webs · Plabic graphs\nSelecta Mathematica (2022) 28:10\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00029-021-00714-6\nSelecta Mathematica\nNew Series\nPromotion of Kreweras words\nSam Hopkins1 · Martin Rubey2\nAccepted: 17 September 2021 / Published online: 26 November 2021\n© The Author(s) 2021\nAbstract\nKreweras words are words consisting of n A’s, n B’s, and n C’s in which every preﬁx\nhas at least as many A’s as B’s and at least as many A’s as C’s. Equivalently, a Kreweras\nword is a linear extension of the poset V × [n]. Kreweras words were introduced in\n1965 by Kreweras, who gave a remarkable product formula for their enumeration.\nSubsequently they became a fundamental example in the theory of lattice walks in the\nquarter plane. We study Schützenberger’s promotion operator on the set of Kreweras\nwords. In particular, we show that 3n applications of promotion on a Kreweras word\nmerely swaps the B’s and C’s. Doing so, we provide the ﬁrst answer to a question\nof Stanley from 2009, asking for posets with ‘good’ behavior under promotion, other\nthan the four families of shapes classiﬁed by Haiman in 1992. We also uncover a\nstrikingly simple description of Kreweras words in terms of Kuperberg’s sl3-webs,\nand Postnikov’s trip permutation associated with any plabic graph. In this description,\nSchützenberger’s promotion corresponds to rotation of the web.\nKeywords Kreweras words/walks · Promotion · Evacuation · Webs · Plabic graphs\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcation 05E18 · 06A07 · 05E10\n1 Introduction\nThe famous ballot problem, whose history stretches back to the 19th century, asks in\nhow many ways we can order the ballots of an election between two candidates Alice\nSam Hopkins was supported by NSF Grant #1802920. Martin Rubey was supported by the the Austrian\nScience Fund (FWF): P 29275.\nB Martin Rubey\nmartin.rubey@tuwien.ac.at\nSam Hopkins\nsamuelfhopkins@gmail.com\n1\nDepartment of Mathematics, Howard University, Washington, DC, USA\n2\nFakultät für Mathematik und Geoinformation, TU Wien, Vienna, Austria--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVolume 17, Number 2, Pages 96–111 (https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/acbf/35ac618195f19c32c694fc192dc99e28c01d.pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; Let λ = (252, 74, 5, 1, 1) be a partition of 84 into parts not ... It is an open conjecture of Bridget Tenner, mentioned in ... lines of Theorem 4.2 can be constructed for general s and t, but Glaisher’s ... ℓ-regular overpartitions, Integers 16 (2016) 8pp, #A37.\nby either t or s. Applying ϕs to any such partition produces a partition into\nparts appearing less than s times, and since part sizes can only be multiplied\nby powers of s, coprime to t, the resulting parts are also not divisible by t.\nHence the maps are onto in both directions.\nApplying instead ϕt, we obtain a partition into parts appearing fewer than\nt times. Again, ϕt multiplies part sizes by powers of t, coprime to s, and\nhence no part is produced divisible by s. The map is onto as above, and so\nwe have the required bijection between the two sets.\n□\nRemark.\nThe proof above required coprimality.\nAn intermediate set—\npartitions into parts divisible by neither s nor t—is equal in size to the\nother two when s and t are coprime, but such a set does not appear when\ngcd(s, t) > 1.\nIt is an open conjecture of Bridget Tenner, mentioned in\n[5], that for s and t not coprime, some map (ϕtϕs−1)k exhibits the required\nbijection. If this conjecture could not be established, another bijection ex-\nhibiting the equality was requested. We believe that a bijection along the\nlines of Theorem 4.2 can be constructed for general s and t, but Glaisher’s\nbijections are an important set of maps in combinatorial partition theory,\nand it would remain interesting to show Tenner’s conjecture independently.\nReferences\n[1] A. M. Alanazi and A. O. Munagi, Combinatorial identities for ℓ-regular overpartitions,\nArs Combin. CXXX (2017), 55–66.\n[2] A. M. Alanazi, A. O. Munagi and J. A. Sellers, An infinite family of congruences for\nℓ-regular overpartitions, Integers 16 (2016) 8pp, #A37.\n[3] B. C. Berndt, Number Theory in the Spirit of Ramanujan, American Mathematical\nSociety, Providence (2006).\n[4] M. D. Hirschhorn and J. A. Sellers, Arithmetic properties of overpartitions into odd\nparts, Ann. Comb. 10 (2006), 353–367.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnumeration of standard barely set-valued tableaux of shifted shapes - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0195669823000446)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Reiner, Tenner and Yong conjectured the following CDE property for a special class of strict partitions. ... Then the interval [0̸,\\lambda ]$[0̸,\\lambda ]$ has the CDE property and \\mathbb{E}\\left(X\\right)=\\mathbb{E}\\left(Y\\right)=\\frac{{\\lambda }_{1}+1}{4}.$\\mathbb{E}\\left(X\\right)=\\mathbb{E}\\left(Y\\right)=\\frac{{\\lambda }_{1}+1}{4}. ... See Fig. 6 for an example. ... Theorem 1.5 [17, Conjecture 2.24]\nReiner, Tenner and Yong conjectured the following CDE property for a special class of strict partitions. Here, {\\delta }_{e}\\left({a}^{a}\\right)${\\delta }_{e}\\left({a}^{a}\\right)$ is the Young diagram obtained from that of {\\delta }_{e}${\\delta }_{e}$ by replacing each cell by the a\\times a$a\\times a$ square \\left({a}^{a}\\right)$\\left({a}^{a}\\right)$, see Fig. 5.\n\nTheorem 1.3 [17, Theorem 2.23]\n\nLet \\lambda $\\lambda $ be the shifted shape {\\delta }_{d}+{\\delta }_{e}\\left({a}^{a}\\right)${\\delta }_{d}+{\\delta }_{e}\\left({a}^{a}\\right)$ with a,d,e\\ge 1$a,d,e\\ge 1$ and d>a(e-1)+1$d>a(e-1)+1$. Then the interval [0̸,\\lambda ]$[0̸,\\l